\section*{Nonzero Constant Term in Majorana Expansion}

For a general two-particle operator in second quantization: 
\begin{equation*}
\hat{O} = \sum_{ijkl} O_{ijkl} \, a_i^\dagger a_j^\dagger a_k a_l, 
\end{equation*}
the Majorana expansion must include a nonzero constant term $C $ because:

\begin{itemize}
\item The trace of $\hat{O}$ is generally non-zero: $\text{Tr}(\hat{O}) \neq
0$. 

\item All non-identity Majorana products are traceless:  
\begin{equation*}
\text{Tr}(\gamma_i\gamma_j) = 0 \quad (i \neq j), \quad \text{Tr}%
(\gamma_i\gamma_j\gamma_k\gamma_l) = 0 \quad (\text{distinct } i,j,k,l).  
\end{equation*}

\item Therefore, $C $ must account for the trace:  
\begin{equation*}
C = \frac{1}{2^N}\text{Tr}(\hat{O}) \quad \text{(for } N \text{ fermionic
modes)}.  
\end{equation*}
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Explicit Form of the Constant Term}

The constant term comes from the \textit{fully contracted} part of the
Majorana expansion. Substituting the Majorana representation: 
\begin{equation*}
a_i^\dagger a_j^\dagger a_k a_l = \frac{1}{16} (\gamma_{2i-1} -
i\gamma_{2i})(\gamma_{2j-1} - i\gamma_{2j})(\gamma_{2k-1} +
i\gamma_{2k})(\gamma_{2l-1} + i\gamma_{2l}), 
\end{equation*}
the constant arises from terms where all four Majorana operators are paired
(i.e., $\gamma_\mu^2 = 1$):

\begin{equation*}
C=\frac{1}{8}\sum_{i,j}O_{ijji}.
\end{equation*}

\subsection*{Complete Majorana Expansion}

The full expansion is: 
\begin{equation*}
\hat{O} = C + \sum_{i < j} \alpha_{ij} \gamma_i \gamma_j + \sum_{i < j < k <
l} \beta_{ijkl} \gamma_i \gamma_j \gamma_k \gamma_l, 
\end{equation*}
where: 
\begin{align*}
C &= \frac{1}{16} \sum_{i,j} \left( O_{ijji} - O_{ijij} \right), \\
\alpha_{ij} &= \frac{1}{4} \sum_{k,l} \left( O_{iklj} - O_{ilkj} + \text{%
cross terms} \right), \\
\beta_{ijkl} &= \frac{1}{16} \sum_{\sigma \in S_4} \text{sgn}(\sigma) \,
O_{\sigma(p)\sigma(q)\sigma(r)\sigma(s)}.
\end{align*}

